<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Griots</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Griots are the hereditary oral historians, praise singers, storytellers and musicians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, who preserve genealogies and historical traditions; among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande peoples</a> they are the jeli or jali. Historically an endogamous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, they served higher-ranking families of warrior kings or emperors, advising them and mediating in disputes, yet were held apart: in the worst case they were buried in trees, not the ground, to avoid polluting the earth. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire</a> they were part of the caste-based social order. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a> names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balla-fasseke">Balla Fasséké</a> as the griot given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a> as adviser, the founder of the Kouyaté line of griots, which still exists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/griots.jpg" alt="Griots" width="250" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeanniot (grav.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GriotF%C3%AAte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A griot is a West African historian, storyteller, praise singer, poet or musician, a master of communicating stories and history orally. The word may derive from the French guiriot, a transliteration of the Portuguese criado, 'servant', and its origin is hard to pin down, hence the many local terms, such as jeli or jali among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manding</a> and gewel among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a>. Jali comes from a root meaning blood. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghan-kon-fatta">Naré Maghann Konaté</a> offered his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, a griot, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balla-fasseke">Balla Fasséké</a>, as adviser. Balla Fasséké is considered the founder of the Kouyaté line of griots, which exists to this day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Historically griots form an endogamous, professionally specialised group, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, whose members mostly marry other griots and pass the tradition down the family line. A family of griots accompanied a family of kings or emperors, called jatigi; in traditional culture no griot can be without a jatigi and no jatigi without a griot, though a jatigi can lend his griot to another. Most villages also had their own griot. Griots sing and share family history at weddings, act as mediators in disputes, and could criticise those of higher status.</p><p>Children born into jeli families are raised from an early age in verbal art, music and genealogy, and learn by observation, repetition and taking part in performances rather than through a classroom. In many performances men narrate the historical framework of epics while women, griottes, sing praise songs, and at weddings griottes dress the bride and sing praises that affirm the bond between the families. Griots have not always been treated positively: Thomas A. Hale writes that an ancient tradition marked them as a separate people and, in the worst case, meant burial in trees rather than in the ground, to avoid polluting the earth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>During the French colonial period griots were increasingly regulated. Colonial authorities in Saint-Louis expelled them from the city, and in 1930 formalised rules for their participation in weddings that limited drumming, set payments and imposed heavy fines. As kings declined, many griots began to work with modern politicians, and after independence they performed in concert halls and recording studios, using microphones, amplifiers and instruments with pickups. Griots today live among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande peoples</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> and many other groups. Some compare rappers to griots, a comparison that is controversial among academics and rappers themselves.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Griot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its early history is known mainly from Bamana oral traditions and the epics of the <b>griots</b>, written down from about 1900 by French colonial officials such as Charles Monteil and later by scholars; contemporary written evidence is thin, and the first detailed eyewitness description is Mungo Park’s, from…</blockquote><a href="/empire/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wolof society distinguished between nobles (garmi or germi), free commoners, endogamous artisan groups such as blacksmiths, leatherworkers and <b>griots</b>, and enslaved people, who formed a significant portion of the population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaabu is remembered above all in Mandinka oral tradition, where <b>griots</b> still recount its history and the fall of Kansala.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Society combined a hereditary aristocracy led by the Keita dynasty with subordinate local chiefs, Muslim scholars and hereditary occupational groups such as smiths and <b>griots</b> (jeli), while most of the population engaged in farming, herding, fishing and craft production.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Beside them stood the nyamakala, hereditary groups of <b>griots</b>, smiths and leatherworkers who married among themselves and were believed to handle a dangerous force, and at the bottom the jon, the enslaved.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>It has been told for generations by <b>griots</b>, or jelis, the traditional oral historians of the Mande world, and it is foundational to Mande culture.</blockquote><a href="/songs/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a></li></ul></section>
